[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the application layer on the remote server (srv1, 10.5.0.4) — specifically the HTTP service. Mechanism: Inter-building network connectivity is intact (ICMP reachability from pc1/pc2/pc3 to 10.5.0.4 succeeds with 0% loss and low RTTs; the core/distribution FRR routers all have OSPF routes to 10.5.0.0/24). However, application connections to the server fail because no web service is listening on the expected ports. Our curl tests from multiple clients to http://10.5.0.4 immediately complete with no payload, and a direct curl with status output from pc2 returns failure. On srv1, netstat/ss show only DNS (tcp/udp 53) and loopback listeners; there is no TCP listener on 80/443. systemctl confirms apache2 is loaded but inactive (dead), and nginx is not running. Firewall is not the blocker (iptables INPUT/FORWARD/OUTPUT are ACCEPT). Localization: srv1 (B-side server), interface eth0 (10.5.0.4), userland daemon. The rest of the inter-office path (dsw/csw) is routing correctly. Root cause: the web server daemon (apache2) on srv1 was stopped/left inactive during last night’s change. Corrective fix: start and enable the HTTP service on srv1 and verify it binds to 10.5.0.4 (and/or 0.0.0.0). Example: systemctl start apache2 && systemctl enable apache2; then validate with curl http://10.5.0.4 from a client and confirm an HTTP 200 response. If TLS is expected, start/enable the HTTPS service and confirm port 443 is listening. No network changes between buildings are required. 
=== END ===